WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.402 --> 00:00:03.737
I can't believe

00:00:03.770 --> 00:00:06.206
that kid that was harming
themselves

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:09.309
would end up sitting here.
I've come so far.

00:00:10.644 --> 00:00:12.746
For the first time,
I'm seeing that

00:00:12.779 --> 00:00:14.448
there's an abundance of
opportunity.

00:00:14.481 --> 00:00:17.651
I wish I wouldn't
have blamed myself so much

00:00:17.684 --> 00:00:20.220
for the way I felt
and for the self-harm,

00:00:20.254 --> 00:00:22.523
because that's made it harder.

00:00:22.556 --> 00:00:25.425
All I actually needed
to do was to be brave enough

00:00:25.459 --> 00:00:26.627
to step out of myself.

00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:30.063
Self-harm behaviours
are warning signs

00:00:30.097 --> 00:00:31.632
that something is not right.

00:00:31.665 --> 00:00:34.401
There's great optimism

00:00:34.434 --> 00:00:36.203
for people making

00:00:36.236 --> 00:00:38.739
a recovery that's good
and that lasts.

00:00:57.224 --> 00:01:00.227
I first sort of
started to self-harm

00:01:00.260 --> 00:01:02.196
when I was about 16.

00:01:02.229 --> 00:01:04.665
It was a lack of confidence

00:01:04.698 --> 00:01:08.168
and sort of feeling like
I didn't fit in anywhere,

00:01:08.202 --> 00:01:10.604
and I just had all this
frustration about

00:01:10.637 --> 00:01:12.673
my identity, who I was.

00:01:12.706 --> 00:01:16.076
My mum was just worried
about me constantly.

00:01:16.109 --> 00:01:18.145
I was not really
talking to anyone about it,

00:01:18.178 --> 00:01:21.481
just assuming this was normal
and everyone felt like that.

00:01:21.515 --> 00:01:22.883
I experienced self-harm

00:01:23.083 --> 00:01:24.318
related to my anger especially.

00:01:24.351 --> 00:01:27.254
From a young age I kind of
learned that anger was

00:01:27.287 --> 00:01:30.724
a scary thing or a thing that I
shouldn't allow myself to feel.

00:01:30.757 --> 00:01:33.327
I experienced trauma
that I blacked out,

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:34.862
that I couldn't access
for years.

00:01:34.895 --> 00:01:37.664
I didn't really know what
I was doing

00:01:37.698 --> 00:01:40.501
or have a plan,
or purpose, even.

00:01:40.534 --> 00:01:42.903
But I was getting desperate.

00:01:52.379 --> 00:01:54.781
Self-harm is a behaviour,

00:01:54.815 --> 00:01:57.184
and it's a form of
communication.

00:01:57.217 --> 00:02:00.854
Self-harm expresses
emotional pain

00:02:00.888 --> 00:02:03.924
through a transformation
into physical pain.

00:02:04.124 --> 00:02:06.226
There are a few statistics

00:02:06.260 --> 00:02:07.661
in relation to self-harm.

00:02:07.694 --> 00:02:10.898
One in six children and
young people aged 16 years

00:02:10.931 --> 00:02:14.868
in England had a problem
with mental illness in 2021.

00:02:14.902 --> 00:02:17.337
That would
translate to something like

00:02:17.371 --> 00:02:19.673
five children in every
classroom of 30.

00:02:24.444 --> 00:02:26.513
You can self-harm
in multiple ways.

00:02:26.547 --> 00:02:27.948
It doesn't always have to be

00:02:28.148 --> 00:02:30.317
through the standard ways
that we know.

00:02:30.350 --> 00:02:32.653
Self-harming relationships,

00:02:32.686 --> 00:02:34.621
substance misuse -
so there's a variety of

00:02:34.655 --> 00:02:36.156
different ways
in which people do it.

00:02:36.190 --> 00:02:38.759
I self-harmed
on and off with cutting,

00:02:38.792 --> 00:02:41.161
then I developed
an eating disorder.

00:02:41.195 --> 00:02:42.629
I just became
very secretive

00:02:42.663 --> 00:02:47.534
and then it sort of turned into
more neglecting myself.

00:02:47.568 --> 00:02:51.905
I'd been out drinking
and I just didn't stop.

00:02:51.939 --> 00:02:53.841
I noticed that
a lot of my anger

00:02:53.874 --> 00:02:54.942
was directed at myself,

00:02:54.975 --> 00:02:56.810
whether it was like
pinching myself

00:02:56.844 --> 00:02:59.246
or biting the inside of
my cheek, things like that.

00:02:59.279 --> 00:03:01.982
A lot of my bingeing would
happen when I was angry

00:03:02.182 --> 00:03:05.552
or upset and didn't feel like
I was being listened to.

00:03:05.586 --> 00:03:07.387
In the very traditional
gender-based ways,

00:03:07.421 --> 00:03:09.656
often males
will tend to self-harm

00:03:09.690 --> 00:03:12.993
in quite violent ways,
so getting into a fight,

00:03:13.193 --> 00:03:15.896
sometimes, or they put
their fist through a glass.

00:03:15.929 --> 00:03:17.998
We're learning a lot more about

00:03:18.198 --> 00:03:19.266
the experience of trauma.

00:03:19.299 --> 00:03:20.601
There's a kind of
capital-T trauma,

00:03:20.634 --> 00:03:22.703
but there's also
small-T trauma.

00:03:22.736 --> 00:03:25.272
All those small wounds
that we all have

00:03:25.305 --> 00:03:27.841
from adverse life experiences

00:03:27.875 --> 00:03:30.844
where perhaps we felt
we haven't been seen or known.

00:03:30.878 --> 00:03:32.513
So it's not that
something happened -

00:03:32.546 --> 00:03:33.947
maybe something didn't happen.

00:03:33.981 --> 00:03:38.418
People know that
it's hard being a teenager.

00:03:38.452 --> 00:03:40.354
It just wasn't that.

00:03:40.387 --> 00:03:43.223
My parents wanted to help,
but they didn't know how,

00:03:43.257 --> 00:03:45.893
so I hid it better. I didn't
want to ask for help

00:03:45.926 --> 00:03:48.462
because I didn't want to seem
like I was attention-seeking,

00:03:48.495 --> 00:03:50.931
or belittled, or made fun of.

00:03:50.964 --> 00:03:53.267
You want to be seen,

00:03:53.300 --> 00:03:56.270
but you don't want attention
for the self-harm.

00:03:56.303 --> 00:03:58.772
When people
say that self-harm

00:03:58.805 --> 00:04:02.943
is attention-seeking is to
see it as attention-needing.

00:04:02.976 --> 00:04:06.747
Because self-harm is
a form of communication,

00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:10.284
in order to move forward,

00:04:10.317 --> 00:04:13.887
you need to find an alternative
form of communication,

00:04:13.921 --> 00:04:18.892
convert it into any other
outward form of communication

00:04:18.926 --> 00:04:21.328
rather than making it implode,

00:04:21.361 --> 00:04:23.931
making the pain
implode back on you.

00:04:23.964 --> 00:04:25.666
Something
I'm doing to work towards

00:04:25.699 --> 00:04:27.868
living a better life
is therapy.

00:04:27.901 --> 00:04:30.537
I still withhold a lot
of myself from other people,

00:04:30.571 --> 00:04:33.407
but I definitely get to
talk to myself

00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:35.409
through the art that I do.

00:04:35.442 --> 00:04:38.345
My anger is a wave
that can come and go.

00:04:38.378 --> 00:04:40.480
Being angry doesn't make me
an angry person.

00:04:40.514 --> 00:04:43.450
I got a journal out
and just started writing.

00:04:43.483 --> 00:04:45.953
I tend to share
a lot of my poetry

00:04:45.986 --> 00:04:47.654
or anything I've written
with my mum.

00:04:48.822 --> 00:04:51.692
Through running
my Instagram account

00:04:51.725 --> 00:04:54.595
and working in the mental
health industry, I think

00:04:54.628 --> 00:04:58.332
it's shown how much helping
other people has helped myself.

00:04:58.365 --> 00:04:59.867
Walking
is a great tool for me.

00:04:59.900 --> 00:05:02.669
Walking allows me to
process things in my mind.

00:05:02.703 --> 00:05:04.972
I do lots of
different things.

00:05:05.005 --> 00:05:08.041
I've had to plan my life
in a way that

00:05:08.075 --> 00:05:10.377
I feel good
and I don't relapse.

00:05:10.410 --> 00:05:13.914
It's important to get your
feelings out in whatever way.

00:05:15.115 --> 00:05:17.718
Creative projects
are really good in order to

00:05:17.751 --> 00:05:21.555
learn how to verbalise things
and understand what's going on.

00:05:21.588 --> 00:05:23.891
Coming together
with a group of people

00:05:23.924 --> 00:05:26.360
that were like myself,
sharing words

00:05:26.393 --> 00:05:29.997
and experiences,
feels really comforting.

00:05:30.030 --> 00:05:32.599
Expressing myself
in a creative way

00:05:32.633 --> 00:05:34.434
using my own words

00:05:34.468 --> 00:05:38.539
I never thought would be
possible for someone like me.

00:05:38.572 --> 00:05:40.007
One thing I've
learnt over the last year or so

00:05:40.040 --> 00:05:41.942
is that it just takes a few
moments of bravery

00:05:41.975 --> 00:05:43.644
to get the support
that you need.

00:05:43.677 --> 00:05:45.546
You don't need to feel
that you deserve it,

00:05:45.579 --> 00:05:47.614
you just need to know
you need it.

00:05:47.648 --> 00:05:49.783
It's not always
going to be perfect,

00:05:49.816 --> 00:05:52.419
but just do your best.

00:05:52.452 --> 00:05:54.955
No matter what you do,

00:05:54.988 --> 00:05:56.890
people are there
to support you.

00:05:56.924 --> 00:06:00.894
We definitely need to be open
for people to

00:06:00.928 --> 00:06:02.763
tell us what they need.

00:06:02.796 --> 00:06:04.398
Society just needs to be

00:06:04.431 --> 00:06:07.868
a lot more person-centred
and trauma-informed.

00:06:07.901 --> 00:06:09.903
Being trauma-aware,

00:06:09.937 --> 00:06:11.071
we have responsibility

00:06:11.104 --> 00:06:14.007
with the young people to relate
to them where they are.

00:06:14.041 --> 00:06:16.510
Having some compassion
that somebody may have

00:06:16.543 --> 00:06:19.613
experienced some trauma
is helpful.

00:06:19.646 --> 00:06:21.582
It's really important
to see this

00:06:21.615 --> 00:06:25.185
as a period of time
in your life which you marked

00:06:25.385 --> 00:06:27.454
as being distressing,

00:06:27.487 --> 00:06:30.891
and to be able to see that
as a phase that you have now

00:06:30.924 --> 00:06:35.128
moved from, and to be proud
of having got through it.

00:06:35.162 --> 00:06:36.830
You can do it.

00:06:36.864 --> 00:06:39.166
I thought that was it.

00:06:39.199 --> 00:06:41.101
Whatever I was feeling,
wherever I was then

00:06:41.134 --> 00:06:42.870
was going to be forever.

00:06:42.903 --> 00:06:44.171
And it wasn't.

00:06:44.204 --> 00:06:47.140
It hasn't been easy
every step of the way,

00:06:47.174 --> 00:06:51.078
growing up, or as an adult,
but it is not the same.

00:06:51.111 --> 00:06:55.215
Things change, and they get
better, and, er...

00:06:56.850 --> 00:06:58.852
...come and go.

00:07:00.821 --> 00:07:03.490
(MUSIC PLAYING)

